{"name":"Poetry in Motion","hex":"#c2aca5","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"poetry in motion","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Fluid, graceful movement. .mw-parser-output .defdate{font-size:smaller}","A person or thing that moves in a particularly fluid, graceful way."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/poetry_in_motion"}],"entries":[{"title":"Poetry in Motion (song)","blocks":[{"text":"\"Poetry in Motion\" is a song recorded by Johnny Tillotson and released on Cadence Records in September 1960."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/2/27/Poetry_In_Motion_Cadence_Records.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Poetry_in_Motion_(song)","wikidata":"Q7207531"},{"title":"Poetry in Motion (film)","blocks":[{"text":"Poetry in Motion is a 1982 Canadian documentary film directed by Ron Mann featuring contemporary North American poetry and music. Featured are some of the Black Mountain poets, Beats, minimalist poets, and avant-garde poets. It was released in theaters, later being distributed on VHS, LaserDisc, and DVD. An extended CD-ROM version was also released."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/d/db/Poetry_in_Motion.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Poetry_in_Motion_(film)","wikidata":"Q7207526"},{"title":"Poetry","blocks":[{"text":"Poetry is a form of literary art that uses aesthetic and often rhythmic qualities of language to evoke meanings in addition to, or in place of, literal or surface-level meanings. A particular instance of poetry is a poem and is written by a poet. Poets use a variety of techniques called poetic devices, such as assonance, alliteration, consonance, euphony and cacophony, onomatopoeia, rhythm, rhyme schemes and sound symbolism, to produce musical or other artistic effects. They also frequently organize these devices into poetic structures, which may be strict or loose, conventional or invented by the poet. Poetic structures vary dramatically by language and cultural convention, but they often rely on rhythmic metre: patterns of syllable stress or syllable weight. They may also use repeating patterns of phonemes, phoneme groups, tones, words, or entire phrases. Poetic structures may even be semantic."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Poetry","wikidata":"Q482"},{"title":"Motion","blocks":[{"text":"In physics, motion is the change in position of an object or fluid with respect to a reference frame over a given time. Motion is mathematically described in terms of vector quantities such as displacement, velocity, acceleration, etc. The relative motion of an object with respect to an observer is the object's motion described in the observer's comoving frame, quantified in terms of relative position, relative velocity, etc. The branch of physics describing the motion of objects without reference to their cause is called kinematics, while the branch studying forces and their effect on motion is called dynamics."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/65/Porsche_race_car_Verschuur_amk.jpg/330px-Porsche_race_car_Verschuur_amk.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Motion","wikidata":"Q79782"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f6/Books_and_Scroll_Ornament_with_Open_Book.png/500px-Books_and_Scroll_Ornament_with_Open_Book.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f6/Books_and_Scroll_Ornament_with_Open_Book.png/500px-Books_and_Scroll_Ornament_with_Open_Book.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABooks_and_Scroll_Ornament_with_Open_Book.png"},{"srcse